      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Deepen: one-word times",
        "body": "Today we deepen our work with simple Spanish tenses. A simple tense uses one verb word and no form of haber. A verb names an action, conjugating changes it for person and time, the stem is the part that stays, and the ending is the part that changes.\n\nUse «cantar» to review the five times. «Canto» is present, «cantaba» is an ongoing or repeated past, «canté» is a finished past, «cantaré» is future, and «cantaría» is conditional. Compare «Cuando era niño cantaba» with «Ayer canté»: the first describes a past habit; the second closes the action.\n\nPractice: Choose one verb and write five sentences for today, when I was little, yesterday, tomorrow, and if I could. Name each tense and explain why it fits.\n\nCommon mistake: Writing «Ayer iba al cine» when you mean that the trip happened and ended. Write «Ayer fui al cine»."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "Simple tenses use one verb word to show the present, an ongoing past, a finished past, the future, or a possible action."
  },
